    <node id="&quot;CONTRASTIVE LEARNING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A machine learning technique that trains models by comparing similar inputs (positive pairs) with dissimilar ones to learn invariant representations."&lt;SEP&gt;"A method used in machine learning which encourages the learning of representations by contrasting positive pairs with negative ones."&lt;SEP&gt;"Contrastive learning involves leveraging co-occurring views (like images with their descriptions) to learn representations by maximizing the similarity between related data points while minimizing that for dissimilar ones."&lt;SEP&gt;"Contrastive learning involves using positive and negative pairs to learn feature representations that distinguish between similar (positive) and different (negative) data points."&lt;SEP&gt;"Contrastive learning maps similar datapoints to similar embeddings and is more closely related to clustering."&lt;SEP&gt;"Contrastive learning represents an alternative strategy that focuses on making representations invariant to certain transformations, allowing for efficient information extraction and compression."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process of contrastive learning involves using different transformations or views of a single data tensor as a way to measure similarities and differences."&lt;SEP&gt;"Contrastive Learning is an alternative training technique used to ensure that similar data points have close embeddings, helping counteract representational collapse."&lt;SEP&gt;"Contrastive learning" is a machine learning technique which aims to learn representations by distinguishing between similar and dissimilar examples."&lt;SEP&gt;"The concept of contrastive learning involves aligning co-occurring sensory signals, as shown in Figure 2."&lt;SEP&gt;"A method used to learn representations from unlabelled data by contrasting similarities within the same class with differences between classes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Contrastive Learning is a dimensionality reduction technique that learns compressed representations by emphasizing differences between similar data points."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c0d666dc2b38320dbc3e9ac039241910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-114ad13da3f029831d24b941874b57f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0b14070bcd3ef6a571246548a4ac62f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbbffe94e6225bcf5568fe62c9c2cc2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89614d99cc7b78ccf3cdf366b2958e93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d51ce3fbf4b4cbff3abd80c83b13839&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-124fbb1b8e31d8533b54ab785a1c693b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-575b5e50472741efa7a02966c34ea787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5edf01cab6c5efd1ae75e8d20f662d03</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 23}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 61}]</data>
    </node>
