    <node id="&quot;ALIGNMENT LOSS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A term that describes a loss function used for learning an encoder that remains invariant to certain transformations. In this context, it is used alongside contrastive learning."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alignment loss is a component used in coupling with another loss to ensure embeddings of certain data points align in a specific space."&lt;SEP&gt;"alignment loss refers to a method used during learning to ensure encoder outputs are invariant to transformations."&lt;SEP&gt;"The concept refers to a type of loss function used in machine learning that aims for alignment or similarity between encoded representations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alignment loss" refers to a term used in learning algorithms that aims to make two representations of the same data agree or align."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b0b14070bcd3ef6a571246548a4ac62f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0d666dc2b38320dbc3e9ac039241910</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 23}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 60}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SECOND LOSS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Second loss is coupled with alignment loss to push apart embeddings for unwanted invariant representations."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c0d666dc2b38320dbc3e9ac039241910</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 28}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SUPERVISION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Supervision for contrastive learning comes in the form of constructing positive and negative pairs from data points."&lt;SEP&gt;"Supervision involves guidance or oversight in the context of prediction tasks and learning algorithms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-114ad13da3f029831d24b941874b57f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0d666dc2b38320dbc3e9ac039241910</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 30}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;\(\MATHBF{X}\)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"\(\mathbf{X}\) is represented as a data tensor that serves as input for various transformations and comparisons."&lt;SEP&gt;"\(\mathbf{x}\) represents a datapoint or input vector used in contrastive learning."&lt;SEP&gt;"\(\mathbf{X}\) represents the total data tensor that is being viewed and transformed in different ways."&lt;SEP&gt;"\(\mathbf{x}\) denotes an input point/vector in some multidimensional space that is subject to transformations and encoding by \(f\)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b0b14070bcd3ef6a571246548a4ac62f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbbffe94e6225bcf5568fe62c9c2cc2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0d666dc2b38320dbc3e9ac039241910</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 11}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 54}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;\(\MATHBF{X}^{+}\)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"VARIABLE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"\(\mathbf{x}^{+}\) is an augmented version of \(\mathbf{x}\) generated by applying transformation \(T\) to ensure alignment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c0d666dc2b38320dbc3e9ac039241910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;\(T()&quot;\)">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Transformation function \(T()\), used in the construction of positive pairs, applies a mapping or transformation to \(\mathbf{x}\)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c0d666dc2b38320dbc3e9ac039241910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;\(\MATHBF{X}^-\)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"VARIABLE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Negative pair \(\mathbf{x}^-\) represents an input vector that should be represented differently from another vector."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c0d666dc2b38320dbc3e9ac039241910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RANDOMLY CONSTRUCTED&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Negative pairs are commonly constructed randomly by selecting two data points for contrastive learning.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c0d666dc2b38320dbc3e9ac039241910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CONCEPT OF INVARIANT REPRESENTATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The concept refers to a stable or unchanging feature representation across different transformations or conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c0d666dc2b38320dbc3e9ac039241910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Z-SPACE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SPACE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"z-space is used in contrastive learning for representing the space where data points' embeddings are compared."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c0d666dc2b38320dbc3e9ac039241910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DATA POINTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Data points used as inputs in contrastive learning tasks, including both \(\mathbf{x}\), its transformations \(\mathbf{x}^{+}\), and negative pairs \(\mathbf{x}^-\)"&lt;SEP&gt;"General term referring to individual pieces of data or observations in machine learning datasets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c0d666dc2b38320dbc3e9ac039241910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0b14070bcd3ef6a571246548a4ac62f</data>
