    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ALIGNMENT LOSS&quot;" target="&quot;LEARNING ENCODER F&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"An alignment loss of form \(\left\lVert f(T(\mathbf{x}))-f(\mathbf{x})\right\rVert_2^2\) is used to learn an invariant encoder \(f\)"</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b0b14070bcd3ef6a571246548a4ac62f</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ALIGNMENT LOSS&quot;" target="&quot;ENCODER&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The encoder 'f' is designed to be invariant to transformations defined by 'T', as enforced by alignment loss."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b0b14070bcd3ef6a571246548a4ac62f</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ALIGNMENT LOSS&quot;" target="&quot;WANG2020HYPERSPHERE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"the concept of alignment loss originates from wang2020hypersphere, as the terminology is attributed to this source."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b0b14070bcd3ef6a571246548a4ac62f</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ALIGNMENT LOSS&quot;" target="&quot;LEARNING ALGORITHM&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"An alignment loss is often part of the objective function in machine learning algorithms, particularly in deep learning models like neural networks."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b0b14070bcd3ef6a571246548a4ac62f</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ALIGNMENT LOSS&quot;" target="&quot;LEARNED ENCODER F&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The alignment loss utilizes a learned encoder \(f\) to make it invariant to transformation \(T\)"</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b0b14070bcd3ef6a571246548a4ac62f</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ALIGNMENT LOSS&quot;" target="&quot;F&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"\(f\) uses an alignment loss in its learning process, aimed at making \(f(T(\mathbf{x}))-f(\mathbf{x})\) as small as possible for transformations \(T\), thereby achieving transformation invariance."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b0b14070bcd3ef6a571246548a4ac62f</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SECOND LOSS&quot;" target="&quot;PUSHING APART OF EMBEDDINGS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The second loss pushes apart the embeddings corresponding to negative pairs, ensuring they represent different features in z-space."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c0d666dc2b38320dbc3e9ac039241910</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SUPERVISION&quot;" target="&quot;TASK&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The task requires supervision in the form of predicting future frames or pixels."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-114ad13da3f029831d24b941874b57f7</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;\(\MATHBF{X}\)&quot;" target="&quot;V_1(&lt;\MATHBF{X}&gt;)&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"\(\mathbf{X}\) is the input for V_1 transformation, generating alternative views of the same data."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cfbbffe94e6225bcf5568fe62c9c2cc2</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;\(\MATHBF{X}\)&quot;" target="&quot;VIEWS OF \(\MATHBF{X}\)&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"\(\mathbf{x}\) and \(\mathbf{y}\) are considered as two different views that result from applying \(V_1\) on \(\mathbf{X}\), possibly based on sensory co-occurrences."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cfbbffe94e6225bcf5568fe62c9c2cc2</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;\(\MATHBF{X}\)&quot;" target="&quot;TRANSFORMATIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Transformations are applied to \(\mathbf{X}\), allowing for different representations and insights to be extracted from it."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cfbbffe94e6225bcf5568fe62c9c2cc2</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;DATA POINTS&quot;" target="&quot;REPRESENTATIONAL COLLAPSE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Data points are affected by representational collapse, potentially leading to all being mapped to identical or similar vectors if such a phenomenon occurs during machine learning model training."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b0b14070bcd3ef6a571246548a4ac62f</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
